WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Tooling is part of secure C programming.

2
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:11.000
The compiler, linker, static analyzer,
sanitizer runtime, dependency scanner,

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.531
and build system all provide signals about
the code.

4
00:00:15.611 --> 00:00:22.288
Ignoring those signals turns preventable
issues into production surprises.

5
00:00:22.368 --> 00:00:29.449
Enable strong compiler warnings and treat
important warnings as work items rather

6
00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.552
than background noise.

7
00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:38.511
A warning about conversion, uninitialized
use, truncation, unreachable code,

8
00:00:38.591 --> 00:00:45.471
or suspicious pointer behavior is often
pointing at an assumption that deserves

9
00:00:45.551 --> 00:00:46.259
review.

10
00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:52.712
Static analysis can find risky patterns,
resource leaks, missing checks,

11
00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:59.570
suspicious conversions, null handling
issues, and inconsistent error paths.

12
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.202
Static analysis is not a replacement for
code review,

13
00:01:04.282 --> 00:01:11.060
but it is useful because it can inspect
paths and patterns that humans may miss in

14
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.556
routine review.

15
00:01:12.636 --> 00:01:18.504
Sanitizers in development and test
environments can catch address,

16
00:01:18.584 --> 00:01:23.945
undefined behavior, memory, and leak
issues where supported.

17
00:01:24.025 --> 00:01:29.994
They are especially valuable for tests
that exercise boundary cases,

18
00:01:30.074 --> 00:01:33.008
failure paths, and unusual input.

19
00:01:33.088 --> 00:01:38.551
Sanitizer findings should be investigated
as design feedback,

20
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.767
not treated as noisy test artifacts.

21
00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:46.702
Dependency review matters for third-party
C libraries.

22
00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:53.560
Track versions, maintenance status,
vulnerability advisories, build flags,

23
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.710
optional features, licenses, and
platform-specific configuration.

24
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:05.354
A small library can bring native code,
transitive dependencies,

25
00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:09.986
and compile-time assumptions into the
application.

26
00:02:10.066 --> 00:02:13.607
Separate debug and production behavior.

27
00:02:13.687 --> 00:02:18.341
Diagnostic output, assertions, core dumps,
tracing,

28
00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:25.401
and verbose logging can help in controlled
environments but may expose sensitive

29
00:02:25.481 --> 00:02:30.539
data or internal details if enabled
broadly in production.

30
00:02:30.619 --> 00:02:34.666
Use hardening options, platform
protections,

31
00:02:34.746 --> 00:02:41.220
and safe build defaults where available,
while still writing correct code.
